<?php

// Curated Vault AI Interview questions: ASP.NET Core (stack key aspnet_core, role backend). 3 series x 3 sets x 5 questions.
// Set ids are {role}-{stack}-s{series}-set{set}. Loaded by database/seeders/CuratedSetQuestionsSeeder (glob) and by the
// 2026_11_19_100000 migration, both as idempotent upserts.

$t = static fn (string $text, string $note, string $category = 'technical'): array => [
    'text' => $text, 'category' => $category, 'duration_seconds' => 180, 'note' => $note,
];

return [

    // ───────────────────────── Series 1 — foundations ─────────────────────────

    'backend-aspnet_core-s1-set1' => [
        $t('Describe the ASP.NET Core request pipeline. What is middleware, how do Use, Run and Map differ, and why does the order of UseRouting, UseAuthentication and UseAuthorization matter?',
            'The pipeline is a chain of middleware delegates; each can act before and after the next one and can short-circuit the request. Use passes control on, Run terminates the pipeline, Map branches by path. Order matters: routing must select the endpoint first, authentication must establish the user, and authorization then evaluates the endpoint policy, so a wrong order leaves endpoints unprotected or breaks identity.'),
        $t('Explain the three service lifetimes in ASP.NET Core dependency injection: Transient, Scoped and Singleton. What is a captive dependency and why is it a bug?',
            'Transient creates a new instance each time, Scoped one per request scope, Singleton one for the application. A captive dependency is a longer-lived service holding a shorter-lived one (for example a singleton injecting a scoped DbContext), keeping it alive across requests and causing stale data, concurrency bugs and memory leaks. Scope validation in development detects it.'),
        $t('Compare controllers, minimal APIs and Razor Pages. When would you choose each for a new project?',
            'Controllers (MVC) offer filters, model binding conventions and structure for larger APIs; minimal APIs are lightweight, fast to write and good for microservices and small endpoints, with endpoint filters and route groups to organise them; Razor Pages suit page-focused server-rendered sites. Choose by team conventions, size and need for filters and conventions; they can coexist.'),
        $t('How does routing work in ASP.NET Core? Explain endpoint routing, attribute routes, route constraints and the main model binding sources such as FromBody, FromQuery and FromRoute.',
            'Endpoint routing matches the request to an endpoint early (UseRouting) and executes it later (UseEndpoints or MapControllers). Attribute routes such as [HttpGet("orders/{id:int}")] define templates with constraints. Model binding fills parameters from route values, query string, headers, form or body; [ApiController] infers sources (complex types from body, simple from route or query).'),
        $t('How does configuration work in ASP.NET Core? Explain appsettings files, environments, environment variables, user secrets and the IOptions, IOptionsSnapshot and IOptionsMonitor interfaces.',
            'Configuration providers are layered (appsettings.json, appsettings.{Environment}.json, user secrets in development, environment variables, command line) with later sources overriding earlier ones. The options pattern binds sections to typed classes: IOptions is a singleton read once, IOptionsSnapshot is scoped per request and reloads, IOptionsMonitor is a singleton that notifies on changes. Secrets must never be committed to source control.'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s1-set2' => [
        $t('What does the [ApiController] attribute change? How should an API action return results and errors, and what is ProblemDetails?',
            '[ApiController] adds automatic model validation responses (400), binding source inference and attribute routing requirements. Actions should return ActionResult<T> or typed results with correct status codes (200, 201 with Location, 204, 400, 404). ProblemDetails (RFC 7807) is a standard JSON error format with type, title, status and detail that clients can parse consistently.'),
        $t('How do you validate input in an ASP.NET Core API? Compare DataAnnotations with FluentValidation, and explain why you use DTOs instead of binding directly to entities (over-posting).',
            'DataAnnotations are attributes on models; FluentValidation offers richer, testable rules in separate classes. Binding directly to EF entities allows over-posting, where a client sets properties it should not (IsAdmin, price), so use request DTOs with only the allowed fields and map explicitly. Validate on the server regardless of client-side checks.'),
        $t('What is a DbContext in Entity Framework Core? What lifetime should it have in a web app, and what do change tracking, AsNoTracking and SaveChanges do?',
            'DbContext is a unit of work and a session with the database that tracks entities and translates LINQ to SQL. It is not thread-safe and is registered Scoped, one per request. Change tracking detects modified entities so SaveChanges writes one transaction; AsNoTracking skips tracking for read-only queries and is faster with lower memory use.'),
        $t('What is the N+1 query problem in EF Core? Compare Include, lazy loading, explicit loading and projection with Select, and say when AsSplitQuery helps.',
            'N+1 happens when loading a list triggers one extra query per item (often via lazy loading in a loop). Include loads related data with joins, projection with Select fetches only the columns needed and is usually the best, explicit loading is manual, lazy loading hides the cost. Large Includes of collections can produce huge cartesian joins, which AsSplitQuery avoids by issuing separate queries.'),
        $t('Why should controller actions be async? Explain how Kestrel and the thread pool serve requests and what happens when you block threads with synchronous calls.',
            'Async I/O frees the thread while waiting for the database or HTTP calls, letting the same threads serve many more requests. Kestrel uses the thread pool; blocking calls (.Result, Thread.Sleep, synchronous I/O) hold threads, and under load the pool starves, latency grows and requests time out. Use async APIs through the whole call chain.'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s1-set3' => [
        $t('Explain authentication versus authorization in ASP.NET Core. Compare cookie authentication with JWT bearer tokens and describe roles, claims and policies.',
            'Authentication establishes who the user is; authorization decides what they may do. Cookies suit server-rendered apps in a browser (with CSRF protection); JWT bearer tokens suit APIs and mobile or SPA clients. A ClaimsPrincipal holds claims; roles are a claim type; policies combine requirements and handlers for richer rules, applied with [Authorize(Policy = "...")].'),
        $t('Which common web vulnerabilities must an ASP.NET Core developer defend against? Cover CSRF, XSS, SQL injection and CORS misconfiguration.',
            'CSRF is mitigated with antiforgery tokens for cookie-based apps; XSS by Razor automatic HTML encoding and never rendering untrusted input raw plus a Content Security Policy; SQL injection by parameterised queries (EF Core LINQ, FromSqlInterpolated) rather than string concatenation; CORS by allowing only specific trusted origins instead of any origin with credentials.'),
        $t('How should passwords and secrets be handled? Explain ASP.NET Core Identity password hashing and where connection strings and API keys should live in development and production.',
            'Never store passwords in plain text or with fast hashes; ASP.NET Core Identity PasswordHasher uses salted PBKDF2 with many iterations and supports rehashing. Secrets go in user secrets locally and in environment variables or a secret manager (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager) in production, ideally with managed identity so no secret is stored in the app at all.'),
        $t('How do you handle errors and logging in ASP.NET Core? Explain UseExceptionHandler, ILogger levels, structured logging and health check endpoints.',
            'UseExceptionHandler (or a global exception handler returning ProblemDetails) converts unhandled exceptions into safe responses without leaking stack traces. ILogger with levels (Trace to Critical) and message templates produces structured logs that can be filtered and searched. Health checks expose liveness and readiness endpoints for load balancers and orchestrators.'),
        $t('How do you host an ASP.NET Core app in production? Explain Kestrel behind a reverse proxy such as IIS, Nginx or a cloud load balancer, forwarded headers, HTTPS and HSTS, and packaging with Docker.',
            'Kestrel is the built-in server and is commonly placed behind a reverse proxy for TLS termination, load balancing and static content. Use the forwarded headers middleware so scheme and client IP are correct behind the proxy, enforce HTTPS redirection and HSTS, and publish a self-contained or framework-dependent build, often as a multi-stage Docker image running as a non-root user.'),
    ],

    // ───────────────────────── Series 2 — applied depth ─────────────────────────

    'backend-aspnet_core-s2-set1' => [
        $t('Compare custom middleware, MVC filters and endpoint filters. Which would you use for logging, authorization, validation and response shaping, and why?',
            'Middleware runs for every request and knows nothing about the selected action, ideal for cross-cutting concerns such as logging, correlation IDs, exception handling and compression. MVC filters run inside the MVC pipeline with access to action arguments, model state and results, good for validation, auditing and response shaping per action. Endpoint filters give minimal APIs the same idea. Use the narrowest scope that has the context you need.'),
        $t('Describe advanced dependency injection features: factory registrations, open generics, keyed services in .NET 8 and resolving scoped services inside a singleton or BackgroundService with IServiceScopeFactory.',
            'Factories create services with runtime logic; open generic registration (IRepository<> to Repository<>) covers many closed types; keyed services let you register several implementations under a key. Singletons and hosted services must create their own scope with IServiceScopeFactory to use scoped services such as DbContext. Enable ValidateScopes and ValidateOnBuild to catch lifetime errors early.'),
        $t('Why should you use IHttpClientFactory instead of new HttpClient() everywhere? Explain socket exhaustion, DNS changes, typed clients and resilience policies such as retry and circuit breaker.',
            'Creating and disposing HttpClient per request exhausts sockets (TIME_WAIT) while a single static one ignores DNS changes. IHttpClientFactory pools and rotates handlers, supports named and typed clients and integrates resilience (Polly or Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience) with timeouts, retries with backoff and circuit breakers so one failing dependency does not take down your service.'),
        $t('How do hosted services and BackgroundService work? How would you run a queue-processing worker inside an ASP.NET Core app and ensure a graceful shutdown?',
            'A BackgroundService runs ExecuteAsync for the lifetime of the host. For a queue worker use a Channel or an external queue, loop on the stoppingToken, create a scope per message, handle exceptions so the loop does not die, and respect the shutdown timeout so in-flight work completes or is safely retried. Consider a separate worker process for heavy workloads.'),
        $t('How do model binding and minimal API parameter binding work, and how would you implement a custom binder or a reusable endpoint filter for validation?',
            'Model binders convert request data to parameters (IModelBinder, custom binder providers); minimal APIs bind from route, query, headers, body and services, with BindAsync or TryParse for custom types. An endpoint filter wraps the handler and can validate arguments, short-circuit with a 400 ProblemDetails and be applied to a route group for reuse.'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s2-set2' => [
        $t('Design pagination, filtering and versioning for a public REST API. Compare offset and keyset pagination and explain how ETags and RowVersion help with caching and concurrency.',
            'Offset pagination (skip/take) is simple but slow and unstable on large or changing tables; keyset pagination uses the last seen key and stays fast and consistent. Version the API by URL, header or media type and keep old versions working. ETags allow conditional requests (304) and If-Match checks, while an EF Core concurrency token such as RowVersion detects conflicting updates.'),
        $t('How do transactions work in EF Core? Explain SaveChanges atomicity, explicit transactions, execution strategies with retries and DbContext pooling.',
            'SaveChanges wraps all pending changes in one transaction. Use BeginTransaction for several SaveChanges or mixed commands. With a retrying execution strategy (transient failure resilience for cloud SQL), wrap the whole unit of work in strategy.ExecuteAsync so it can be retried safely. DbContext pooling reuses context instances to cut allocation cost, so no per-request state must be stored in the context.'),
        $t('A list endpoint is slow. Which EF Core query optimisations do you apply: indexes, projections, AsNoTracking, split queries, compiled queries and ExecuteUpdate or ExecuteDelete for bulk operations?',
            'Look at the generated SQL and execution plan first; add the right indexes, project only needed columns into DTOs, use AsNoTracking for reads, avoid client-side evaluation and loading whole collections, use AsSplitQuery for large includes, compiled queries on hot paths, and ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete to change many rows without loading entities.'),
        $t('How do you run EF Core migrations safely in production? Discuss idempotent SQL scripts, migration bundles, backward-compatible (expand and contract) changes and rollback.',
            'Generate reviewed SQL scripts (idempotent) or a migration bundle instead of running Database.Migrate() at app start on many instances. Use expand and contract: add new columns or tables first, deploy code that works with both, backfill, then remove old structures later. Keep a rollback plan, back up before destructive changes and test migrations against production-like data.'),
        $t('Compare IMemoryCache, IDistributedCache with Redis, response caching and output caching. What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it?',
            'IMemoryCache is per instance and fastest but not shared; IDistributedCache (Redis) is shared across instances; response caching follows HTTP headers; output caching in .NET 7 and later caches server-side with tags and policies. A stampede is many requests recomputing an expired key at once; prevent it with locking or single-flight loading, jittered expiry, stale-while-revalidate and pre-warming.'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s2-set3' => [
        $t('Explain JWT validation in depth: signature, issuer, audience, lifetime and clock skew. How do refresh tokens, key rotation and token revocation work, and how do OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect fit in?',
            'The API validates signature with the issuer keys (JWKS), plus issuer, audience, expiry and not-before with a small clock skew. Short-lived access tokens are paired with refresh tokens stored securely and rotated; key rotation uses multiple valid keys during transition; revocation needs a deny list or short lifetimes because JWTs are self-contained. OAuth 2.0 delegates access, OpenID Connect adds identity; use an identity provider rather than building your own.'),
        $t('How does the built-in rate limiting middleware work? Compare fixed window, sliding window, token bucket and concurrency limiters and describe protecting a login endpoint.',
            'Rate limiting policies are registered and applied to endpoints; fixed window counts requests per interval, sliding window smooths the boundary, token bucket allows bursts at a steady refill rate, concurrency limits parallel requests. Partition by user, API key or IP, return 429 with Retry-After, and use stricter limits plus lockout and monitoring on authentication endpoints.'),
        $t('What is the ASP.NET Core Data Protection API and what breaks when you run several instances behind a load balancer without configuring it?',
            'Data Protection encrypts cookies, antiforgery tokens and other payloads with keys from a key ring. With multiple instances or containers each generating its own keys, one instance cannot decrypt what another issued, causing random logouts and antiforgery errors. Persist and share keys (Redis, blob storage, database) and protect them at rest, and set a common application name.'),
        $t('How would you improve the throughput of an ASP.NET Core API? Discuss response compression, System.Text.Json source generation, pooling, avoiding allocations and how to measure with BenchmarkDotNet and dotnet-counters.',
            'Measure first. Enable response compression for text payloads, use source-generated JSON serialisers, pool or reuse expensive objects, reduce allocations on hot paths, stream large results, make I/O async and tune Kestrel limits. Use BenchmarkDotNet for micro-benchmarks, load tests for end-to-end numbers and dotnet-counters or OpenTelemetry metrics in production to confirm the effect.'),
        $t('How do liveness and readiness health checks differ in Kubernetes, and how should an ASP.NET Core app handle graceful termination and configuration in containers?',
            'Liveness tells the orchestrator whether to restart the container; readiness tells it whether to send traffic (for example when the database is unavailable or a warm-up is not finished). Handle SIGTERM through the host shutdown, stop accepting new requests, finish in-flight work within the shutdown timeout, and read configuration from environment variables or mounted secrets rather than baked-in files.'),
    ],

    // ───────────────────────── Series 3 — high-bar scenarios ─────────────────────────

    'backend-aspnet_core-s3-set1' => [
        $t('Compare Clean Architecture, onion and vertical slice organisation in ASP.NET Core. Where do MediatR and CQRS help or hurt, and how do you keep the domain free of framework dependencies?',
            'Layered styles put domain at the centre with application, infrastructure and web on the outside and dependencies pointing inward; vertical slices group code by feature to reduce cross-layer churn. MediatR and CQRS add a clear request and handler model and cross-cutting pipelines, but also indirection and ceremony for simple CRUD. Keep domain types free of EF, HTTP and DI attributes by using interfaces and mapping at the edges.', 'architecture'),
        $t('Design a multi-tenant SaaS API in ASP.NET Core. How do you resolve the tenant, isolate data (database per tenant, schema per tenant or tenant column) and use EF Core global query filters safely?',
            'Resolve the tenant from the subdomain, header or token claim in middleware and make it available through a scoped service. Isolation options trade cost against safety: database per tenant is strongest, a shared database with a TenantId column is cheapest. EF global query filters add the tenant predicate automatically, but you must prevent bypass (IgnoreQueryFilters, raw SQL), test isolation and consider row-level security.', 'architecture'),
        $t('How would you migrate a large legacy ASP.NET Framework (WebForms or MVC 5) application to ASP.NET Core without a big-bang rewrite?',
            'Use the strangler fig approach: put a reverse proxy such as YARP in front, move features to ASP.NET Core one at a time, share authentication and session through compatible mechanisms or the System.Web adapters, extract shared libraries to .NET Standard or modern .NET, add characterisation tests first and migrate the data layer gradually. Plan for removed APIs and third-party dependencies.', 'scenario'),
        $t('When and how would you use YARP or an API gateway or Backend-for-Frontend pattern in an ASP.NET Core system? What cross-cutting concerns belong in the gateway and which do not?',
            'A gateway or BFF gives clients a single entry point, aggregates calls, terminates TLS, handles authentication, rate limiting, routing and caching. Business rules should stay in services, otherwise the gateway becomes a bottleneck and a distributed monolith. Propagate identity and trace context to downstream services and design for the gateway to be horizontally scalable and observable.', 'architecture'),
        $t('A team of ten is building a new product on .NET. How do you decide between a modular monolith and microservices, and when would you choose gRPC or messaging instead of REST between services?',
            'Start with a modular monolith with clear module boundaries and in-process contracts, since microservices add operational and consistency costs that a small team rarely benefits from early. Split out services when scaling, team autonomy or deployment independence demand it. gRPC suits low-latency internal calls with strong contracts; messaging (Service Bus, RabbitMQ, Kafka) suits asynchronous workflows and decoupling; REST suits public APIs.', 'architecture'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s3-set2' => [
        $t('Explain the transactional outbox pattern with EF Core. How does it make sending messages reliable, and why must consumers be idempotent?',
            'Write the business change and an outbox message in the same database transaction, then a background publisher reads unsent outbox rows and publishes them to the broker, marking them sent. This avoids the dual-write problem where the database commits but the message is lost (or the reverse). Delivery is at least once, so consumers must tolerate duplicates using idempotency keys or deduplication.', 'architecture'),
        $t('Two users edit the same record at the same time. How do optimistic concurrency and concurrency tokens in EF Core detect the conflict, and how should the API and UI resolve it?',
            'A RowVersion or concurrency token column is included in the UPDATE condition; if another user changed the row, zero rows are affected and EF throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException. The API returns 409 or 412, the client can reload the latest values and show a merge or overwrite choice. Use pessimistic locks only when conflicts are frequent and costly.', 'scenario'),
        $t('How would you support uploading and downloading very large files in ASP.NET Core without loading them fully into memory? Consider request size limits, streaming, MultipartReader and object storage.',
            'Avoid IFormFile buffering for big files: stream the multipart body with MultipartReader to storage, set explicit request size limits and timeouts, validate type and size and scan for malware, and prefer direct uploads to object storage using pre-signed URLs. For downloads return a Stream with range processing and correct headers so the server does not buffer the whole content.'),
        $t('A specific EF Core query is slow only in production. Describe how you find the generated SQL, analyse the execution plan and decide between index changes, query rewrites, raw SQL and caching.',
            'Log or capture the SQL (EnableSensitiveDataLogging in non-production, logging categories, Application Insights or a database profiler), run it with the production data volume to get the actual execution plan, look for table scans, missing indexes, large sorts and parameter sniffing, then rewrite the LINQ, add indexes, use projections or raw SQL when needed, and cache only when the data allows staleness.', 'scenario'),
        $t('How do you run scheduled and background jobs safely when the application runs on several instances? Compare Hangfire, Quartz.NET and cloud schedulers and discuss distributed locks and idempotency.',
            'Every instance would run the same timer, so use a persistent job store with locking (Hangfire or Quartz clustering) or a cloud scheduler that triggers a single execution. Jobs must be idempotent because they can retry, use distributed locks (database, Redis) for singletons, record job state, and handle shutdowns and poison jobs with retries and dead-letter handling.'),
    ],

    'backend-aspnet_core-s3-set3' => [
        $t('Your API returns timeouts and 500 errors only under heavy load while CPU stays low. Diagnose thread pool starvation: what signs do you look for, which tools do you use and what are the fixes?',
            'Low CPU with growing request latency and queue length points to blocked threads. Use dotnet-counters (thread pool queue length, thread count), dotnet-stack or dumps to find threads blocked on .Result or .Wait or synchronous I/O. Fix by making the code async end to end, removing blocking calls, bounding concurrency to dependencies and only as a stopgap raising the minimum thread count.', 'scenario'),
        $t('An ASP.NET Core service leaks memory over days. Which causes are typical (static caches, HttpClient misuse, event handlers, captured DbContext) and how do you investigate with dotnet-gcdump?',
            'Typical causes: unbounded in-memory caches or static collections, subscribing to events without unsubscribing, singletons capturing scoped services, creating many HttpClient instances or large objects on the LOH, and unbounded queues. Take gcdump snapshots at different times, compare object counts and inspect retention paths to the GC root, then fix the owner and add limits or expiry.', 'scenario'),
        $t('How do you achieve zero-downtime deployments for an ASP.NET Core API with a database? Discuss blue-green or rolling releases, backward-compatible schema changes and feature flags.',
            'Run old and new versions side by side (rolling or blue-green) behind a load balancer with readiness checks and connection draining. Make every database change backward compatible (expand, migrate, contract) so both versions work during the transition, and hide new behaviour behind feature flags that can be turned off without a redeploy. Automate smoke tests and rollbacks.', 'architecture'),
        $t('A database connection string and JWT signing key were accidentally committed to a public repository. Walk through your incident response and how you prevent a recurrence.',
            'Treat both as compromised: rotate the signing key and database credentials immediately, invalidate existing tokens and sessions, check logs and the database for misuse, remove the secrets from history and notify stakeholders as required. Prevent recurrence with secret scanning and pre-commit hooks, a secret manager or Key Vault with managed identities, least-privilege credentials and short-lived tokens.', 'situational'),
        $t('Design observability for a .NET service estate with OpenTelemetry: traces, metrics, logs, correlation across services, sampling and alerting on service level objectives.',
            'Instrument ASP.NET Core, HttpClient and database calls with OpenTelemetry, propagate trace context across services, export to a backend (Jaeger, Tempo, Application Insights, Grafana), add business and runtime metrics, use structured logs correlated by trace ID, apply sampling to control cost and alert on SLO burn rates (error rate and latency percentiles) rather than raw CPU.', 'architecture'),
    ],
];
